# Zero-downtime Postgres migrations

By [Ana Ruiz](/authors/ana) · March 3, 2024 · 8 min read

Every team that runs __Postgres__ in production eventually needs to change a
table that is _too big to lock_. This post walks through the pattern we use at
[Example](https://example.com "Example Inc."), step by step.

## Why `ALTER TABLE` hurts

Most `ALTER TABLE` forms take an __ACCESS EXCLUSIVE__ lock. While it is held,
even a plain `SELECT` waits — and so does every query queued behind it.

> A migration is only as safe as its slowest lock.
>
> — someone on call at 3 a.m.

## The steps

1. Add the new column as `NULL`able, with no default.
2. Backfill in batches:

   ```
   UPDATE orders
   SET    total_cents = total * 100
   WHERE  id BETWEEN $1 AND $2;
   ```
3. Add the constraint as `NOT VALID`, then validate it:
   - `ALTER TABLE orders ADD CONSTRAINT … NOT VALID;`
   - `ALTER TABLE orders VALIDATE CONSTRAINT …;`
4. Switch reads, then writes, behind a [feature flag](/flags).

### What about _indexes_?

Use `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`. It cannot run inside a transaction, so most
migration tools need a flag for it; ours is `disable_ddl_transaction!`.  
Watch `pg_stat_progress_create_index` while it runs.

![A queue of blocked queries](/img/lock-queue.png "Lock queue")

---

Tags: [postgres](/t/postgres), [migrations](/t/migrations).
Questions? Mail [blog@example.com](mailto:blog@example.com) or see
<https://example.com/faq>.
